## Install

Requires Zig {{minimum_zig_version}} or later.

```bash
zig fetch --save git+{{repository}}#v{{version}}
```

Then add the module to your `build.zig`:

```zig
const {{name}} = b.dependency("{{name}}", .{
    .target = target,
    .optimize = optimize,
});
exe.root_module.addImport("{{name}}", {{name}}.module("{{name}}"));
```

## Quickstart

```zig
const std = @import("std");
const sdk = @import("{{name}}");

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !void {
    const token = init.environ_map.get("API_TOKEN") orelse "";

    var client = sdk.init(init.gpa, init.io, token);
    defer client.deinit();

    std.debug.print("Client ready for {s}\n", .{client.base_url});
}
```

## Documentation

The [usage guide](docs/USAGE.md) covers authentication, error handling and the limitations of the generated code.

The [examples](examples) are compiled in CI, and you can run them from a clone of this repository:

```bash
zig build run-quickstart
```

## Development

```bash
zig build test
```

```bash
zig build check
```

```bash
zig build examples
```

`zig build check` compiles every generated operation. Zig normally skips code that nothing calls, so `zig build test` alone won't catch a broken operation.

